    "updated_at": "2024-07-30T22:24:08Z",
    "body": "This comes up extremely frequently in [WebNN](https://webmachinelearning.github.io/webnn/)\r\n\r\n* Parameters like size (width, height), padding (top/bottom/left/right), etc. passed as lists\r\n* A layout parameter (e.g. \"nchw\") guides the unpacking of a list of the dimensions of an input tensor (multidimensional array) into 4 different variables (batches, channels, height, width) - this looks weird at first but this is an industry-standard thing\r\n\r\nI agree that a variation of the tuple syntax would be good. And I like @dlrobertson's suggestion that the LHS should use « » to be a cue that we're talking about a list on the RHS, i.e.:\r\n\r\n> 1. Let « _batchSize_, _inputSize_ » be _inputShape_.\r\n\r\nThe tuple syntax (i.e. parentheses) might be too subtle. Also, since we're dealing with a dynamic sized aggregate here, do we want to require that a size check is done, either via an assertion (if pre-checked somehow), preceding statement, or incorporate it here, e.g.:\r\n\r\n> 1. Let « _batchSize_, _inputSize_ » be _inputShape_, or throw a TypeError if _inputShape_'s size is not 2.\r\n\r\nThat's probably too fancy.",
