    "created_at": "2024-08-07T12:37:52Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-08-07T12:37:52Z",
    "body": "> Also, since we're dealing with a dynamic sized aggregate here, do we want to require that a size check is done, either via an assertion (if pre-checked somehow), preceding statement, or incorporate it here, e.g.:\r\n>\r\n> > 1. Let « batchSize, inputSize » be inputShape, or throw a TypeError if inputShape's size is not 2.\r\n\r\nGood point about list length. I actually do really like this. I like that spec writers are given the chance to provide the error type, and the implementers of the spec can't miss the length check. I worry that hiding the assert in [infra](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#list), may result in someone implementing the spec to miss a length check. I'll post a PR that implements `#list-multiple-assignment` that will contain the assert. If we don't like it, I'm more than happy to go this route.\r\n\r\nThanks for the feedback @inexorabletash!",
